import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Duality.Polar
import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Duality.Support
import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Recession.ConeHull
import Numlib.Analysis.Convex.Recession.Function

/-!
# Recession functions, conjugates and polar cones

Let $E$ and $F$ be real vector spaces with a bilinear pairing $\langle x, y \rangle$, and let
$f^*(y) = \sup_x (\langle x, y \rangle - f(x))$ be the conjugate of a function
$f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$. This module relates the recession data of a function to the
effective domain of its conjugate, in two dual forms. For functions, the recession function of $f^*$
is the support function of $\operatorname{dom} f$:
$$(f^*)0^+ = \delta^*(\cdot \mid \operatorname{dom} f).$$
For cones, the recession cone of $f^*$ is the polar cone of $\operatorname{dom} f$; this is the
first statement read at the level $0$. The presentation follows §13 and §14 of
[rockafellar1970convex].

One inequality, $(f^*)0^+ \le \delta^*(\cdot \mid \operatorname{dom} f)$, holds with no hypothesis,
because the supremum that defines $f^*$ can be bounded term by term. The reverse inequality needs a
point $z$ at which $f^*$ is finite, so that Fenchel's inequality at $z + ay$ can be taken to the
limit $a \to \infty$. This is the hypothesis that $f^*$ is proper, which holds when $f$ is closed
proper convex.

## Main results

* `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_convexConj`,
  `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj`: $(f^*)0^+ = \delta^*(\cdot \mid
  \operatorname{dom} f)$, and, for a closed proper convex $f$, the dual form $f0^+ = \delta^*(\cdot
  \mid \operatorname{dom} f^*)$ (Theorem 13.3 of [rockafellar1970convex]).
* `ConvexAnalysis.constancySpace_convexConj`: the constancy space of $f^*$ is the annihilator $\{y
  \mid \langle x, y \rangle = 0 \text{ for all } x \in \operatorname{dom} f\}$ of
  $\operatorname{dom} f$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_convexConj`, `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_convexConj_hull`,
  `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_eq_polarCone_convexDom_convexConj`,
  `ConvexAnalysis.polarCone_recessionConeFn`: the recession cone of $f^*$ is the polar of
  $\operatorname{dom} f$, and of the convex cone that $\operatorname{dom} f$ generates; for a closed
  proper convex $f$, the recession cone of $f$ is the polar of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$, and its
  polar is the closure of the convex cone generated by $\operatorname{dom} f^*$ (Theorem 14.2 of
  [rockafellar1970convex]).
* `ConvexAnalysis.zero_mem_interior_iff_polarCone_eq_zero`: a nonempty convex set $D$ in a
  finite-dimensional space has $0$ in its interior if and only if its polar cone is $\{0\}$.
* `ConvexAnalysis.isBounded_setOf_le_iff_zero_mem_interior_convexDom_convexConj`: in finite
  dimension, every sublevel set $\{z \mid f(z) \le \alpha\}$ of a closed proper convex $f$ is
  bounded if and only if $0 \in \operatorname{int}(\operatorname{dom} f^*)$.

## Implementation notes

* The pairing is a bilinear map `B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ`. The conjugate is
  `ConvexAnalysis.convexConj B f`, the support function $\delta^*(y \mid s) = \sup_{x \in s} \langle
  x, y \rangle$ is `ConvexAnalysis.supportFn B s`, and the polar cone $\{y \mid \langle x, y \rangle
  \le 0 \text{ for all } x \in K\}$ is `ConvexAnalysis.polarCone B K`. Statements about $f$ itself,
  as the conjugate of $f^*$, use the flipped pairing `B.flip`.
* The results that use $f^{**} = f$ assume that $E$ is a locally convex space and that
  `ConvexAnalysis.IsCompatiblePairing B` holds: every $\langle \cdot, y \rangle$ is continuous on
  $E$, and every continuous linear functional on $E$ is $\langle \cdot, y \rangle$ for some
  $y \in F$.
* The recession cone of a function, $\{y \mid (f0^+)(y) \le 0\}$, is
  `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn`, and the constancy space, the set of $y$ with $(f0^+)(y) \le 0$
  and $(f0^+)(-y) \le 0$, is `ConvexAnalysis.constancySpace`.
* The constancy space of $f^*$ is the form that the image and duality theorems use: the statement
  that $f^*$ is constant along $y$ becomes the statement that $y$ annihilates
  $\operatorname{dom} f$, which a relative interior hypothesis can verify.
* The direct form of the polar statement uses $\operatorname{dom} f$ instead of the convex cone it
  generates; the polar cone does not distinguish the two (`ConvexAnalysis.polarCone_hull`).
-/

open scoped Pointwise

namespace ConvexAnalysis

section RecessionConj

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} {f : E → EReal}

/-- For every function $f$ on $E$,
$$(f^*)0^+ \le \delta^*(\cdot \mid \operatorname{dom} f).$$

The proof bounds the supremum that defines $f^*$ term by term. Off $\operatorname{dom} f$ the
term is $-\infty$, and on $\operatorname{dom} f$ the bound $\langle x, y \rangle \le \nu$ moves
$a\langle x, y \rangle$ past the supremum. -/
theorem recessionFn_convexConj_le_supportFn_convexDom (B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ) (f : E → EReal) :
    recessionFn (convexConj B f) ≤ supportFn B (convexDom f) := by
  intro y
  refine EReal.le_of_forall_coe_le fun ν hν => ?_
  rw [supportFn_le_coe_iff] at hν
  rw [recessionFn_le_coe_iff, mk_mem_recessionCone_epi_iff]
  intro z a ha
  rw [convexConj_apply]
  refine iSup_le fun x => ?_
  by_cases hx : x ∈ convexDom f
  · have hBx : B x (z + a • y) = B x z + a * B x y := by rw [map_add, map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    have hmul : a * B x y ≤ a * ν := mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_left (hν x hx) ha
    have hle : B x (z + a • y) ≤ B x z + a * ν := by rw [hBx]; linarith
    calc ((B x (z + a • y) : ℝ) : EReal) - f x
        ≤ ((B x z + a * ν : ℝ) : EReal) - f x :=
          EReal.sub_le_sub (EReal.coe_le_coe_iff.2 hle) le_rfl
      _ = (((B x z : ℝ) : EReal) - f x) + ((a * ν : ℝ) : EReal) :=
          EReal.coe_add_sub _ _ _
      _ ≤ convexConj B f z + ((a * ν : ℝ) : EReal) :=
          add_le_add (sub_le_convexConj B f x z) le_rfl
  · rw [mem_convexDom, not_lt, top_le_iff] at hx
    rw [hx, EReal.sub_top]
    exact bot_le

/-- If $f$ and $f^*$ are proper, then
$$\delta^*(\cdot \mid \operatorname{dom} f) \le (f^*)0^+.$$

Properness of $f^*$ gives a point $z$ at which $f^*$ is finite. Fenchel's inequality at $z + ay$
gives $\langle x, z \rangle + a\langle x, y \rangle - f(x) \le f^*(z) + a\nu$, and the limit
$a \to \infty$ gives $\langle x, y \rangle \le \nu$. -/
theorem supportFn_convexDom_le_recessionFn_convexConj (hp : ProperConvex f)
    (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    supportFn B (convexDom f) ≤ recessionFn (convexConj B f) := by
  intro y
  refine EReal.le_of_forall_coe_le fun ν hν => ?_
  rw [supportFn_le_coe_iff]
  intro x hx
  obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ := hc.convexDom_nonempty
  obtain ⟨c, hcz⟩ := EReal.exists_coe_of_ne_bot_of_lt_top (hc.ne_bot z) hz
  obtain ⟨r, hrx⟩ := EReal.exists_coe_of_ne_bot_of_lt_top (hp.ne_bot x) hx
  have hrec : ∀ a : ℝ,
      0 ≤ a → convexConj B f (z + a • y) ≤ convexConj B f z + ((a * ν : ℝ) : EReal) :=
    fun a ha => mk_mem_recessionCone_epi_iff.1 (recessionFn_le_coe_iff.1 hν) z a ha
  have hbound : ∀ a : ℝ, 0 ≤ a → B x z + a * B x y - r ≤ c + a * ν := by
    intro a ha
    have hsub := sub_le_convexConj B f x (z + a • y)
    rw [hrx] at hsub
    have hle := hsub.trans (hrec a ha)
    rw [hcz, ← EReal.coe_sub, ← EReal.coe_add, EReal.coe_le_coe_iff] at hle
    have hBx : B x (z + a • y) = B x z + a * B x y := by rw [map_add, map_smul, smul_eq_mul]
    rw [hBx] at hle
    linarith
  by_contra hcon
  push Not at hcon
  have hd : 0 < B x y - ν := by linarith
  set q : ℝ := (c + r - B x z) / (B x y - ν) with hq
  set a : ℝ := max (q + 1) 0 with ha
  have ha0 : 0 ≤ a := le_max_right _ _
  have haq : q < a := lt_of_lt_of_le (by linarith) (le_max_left _ _)
  have hqmul : q * (B x y - ν) = c + r - B x z := by rw [hq, div_mul_cancel₀ _ hd.ne']
  have hb := hbound a ha0
  have hexp : a * (B x y - ν) = a * B x y - a * ν := by ring
  have hstep : a * (B x y - ν) ≤ q * (B x y - ν) := by rw [hqmul, hexp]; linarith
  exact absurd (le_of_mul_le_mul_right hstep hd) (not_le.2 haq)

/-- Let $E$ and $F$ be real vector spaces with a bilinear pairing $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle$,
and let $f : E \to [-\infty, +\infty]$ be a proper function whose conjugate $f^*$ is proper.
Then the recession function of $f^*$ is the support function of the effective domain of $f$:
$$(f^*)0^+ = \delta^*(\cdot \mid \operatorname{dom} f).$$
This is part of Theorem 13.3 of [rockafellar1970convex].

Convexity of $f$ is not assumed. The hypothesis that $f^*$ is proper holds when $f$ is closed
proper convex. -/
theorem recessionFn_convexConj (hp : ProperConvex f) (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    recessionFn (convexConj B f) = supportFn B (convexDom f) :=
  le_antisymm (recessionFn_convexConj_le_supportFn_convexDom B f)
      (supportFn_convexDom_le_recessionFn_convexConj hp hc)

/-- Let $E$ be a locally convex real topological vector space, let the pairing of $E$ and $F$ be
compatible (`ConvexAnalysis.IsCompatiblePairing`), and let $f$ be a closed proper convex
function on $E$. Then the recession function of $f$ is the support function of the effective
domain of $f^*$:
$$f0^+ = \delta^*(\cdot \mid \operatorname{dom} f^*).$$
This is part of Theorem 13.3 of [rockafellar1970convex].

The support function is taken for the flipped pairing `B.flip`. The proof applies
`ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_convexConj` to $f^*$ and uses $f^{**} = f$. -/
theorem recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E]
    [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E] [IsCompatiblePairing B]
    (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    recessionFn f = supportFn B.flip (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := by
  have hbi : convexConj B.flip (convexConj B f) = f := convexBiconj_eq_self hf.convex hf.closed
  have h := recessionFn_convexConj (B := B.flip) (f := convexConj B f) (properConvex_convexConj hf)
    (by rw [hbi]; exact hf.proper)
  rwa [hbi] at h

/-- Let $f$ and $f^*$ be proper. Then the constancy space of $f^*$ is the annihilator of the
effective domain of $f$:
$$\{y \mid (f^*)0^+(y) \le 0 \text{ and } (f^*)0^+(-y) \le 0\} = \{y \in F \mid \langle x, y
  \rangle = 0 \text{ for all } x \in \operatorname{dom} f\}.$$

This is the form that the constancy hypothesis of the image theorem takes for $f^*$: $f^*$ is
constant along $y$ exactly when $\langle x, y \rangle = 0$ for every
$x \in \operatorname{dom} f$. -/
theorem constancySpace_convexConj (hp : ProperConvex f) (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    constancySpace (convexConj B f) = {y : F | ∀ x ∈ convexDom f, B x y = 0} := by
  ext y
  rw [mem_constancySpace, recessionFn_convexConj hp hc, Set.mem_ofPred_eq]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨h₁, h₂⟩
    rw [supportFn_le_zero_iff] at h₁ h₂
    intro x hx
    have hneg := h₂ x hx
    rw [map_neg] at hneg
    linarith [h₁ x hx]
  · intro h
    constructor <;> rw [supportFn_le_zero_iff] <;> intro x hx
    · exact le_of_eq (h x hx)
    · rw [map_neg, h x hx, neg_zero]

end RecessionConj

/-! ### Recession cones and polars of effective domains -/

section RecessionPolar

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} {f : E → EReal}

/-- Let $f$ and $f^*$ be proper. Then the recession cone of $f^*$ is the polar cone of the effective
domain of $f$:
$$\{y \mid (f^*)0^+(y) \le 0\} = \{y \in F \mid \langle x, y \rangle \le 0 \text{ for all } x
  \in \operatorname{dom} f\}.$$

The statement uses $\operatorname{dom} f$ instead of the convex cone it generates; the polar
cone does not distinguish them, and `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_convexConj_hull` is the form
with the cone. The proof reads `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_convexConj` at the level $0$:
$(f^*)0^+(y) \le 0$ says that $\langle x, y \rangle \le 0$ for every
$x \in \operatorname{dom} f$. -/
theorem recessionConeFn_convexConj (hp : ProperConvex f) (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    recessionConeFn (convexConj B f) = polarCone B (convexDom f) := by
  ext y
  rw [mem_recessionConeFn, recessionFn_convexConj hp hc, supportFn_le_zero_iff]
  exact Iff.rfl

/-- Let $f$ and $f^*$ be proper. Then the polar of the convex cone generated by
$\operatorname{dom} f$ is the recession cone $\{y \mid (f^*)0^+(y) \le 0\}$ of $f^*$. This is
part of Theorem 14.2 of [rockafellar1970convex]. -/
theorem recessionConeFn_convexConj_hull (hp : ProperConvex f) (hc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f)) :
    polarCone B (PointedCone.hull ℝ (convexDom f) : Set E) = recessionConeFn (convexConj B f) := by
  rw [polarCone_hull, recessionConeFn_convexConj hp hc]

end RecessionPolar

section RecessionPolarDual

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] {f : E → EReal}

/-- Let $E$ be a locally convex real topological vector space, let the pairing of $E$ and $F$ be
compatible, and let $f$ be a closed proper convex function on $E$. Then the recession cone of
$f$ is the polar cone of the effective domain of $f^*$:
$$\{x \mid (f0^+)(x) \le 0\} = \{x \in E \mid \langle x, y \rangle \le 0 \text{ for all } y \in
  \operatorname{dom} f^*\}.$$

This is `ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj` read at the level $0$, in
the same way as `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_convexConj` reads
`ConvexAnalysis.recessionFn_convexConj`. The polar cone is taken for the flipped pairing
`B.flip`. The existence theory for minimizers uses this form. -/
theorem recessionConeFn_eq_polarCone_convexDom_convexConj (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    recessionConeFn f = polarCone B.flip (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := by
  ext y
  rw [mem_recessionConeFn, recessionFn_eq_supportFn_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf,
      supportFn_le_zero_iff]
  exact Iff.rfl

end RecessionPolarDual

section RecessionPolarBipolar

variable {E F : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [AddCommGroup F] [Module ℝ F]
  [TopologicalSpace E] [IsTopologicalAddGroup E] [ContinuousSMul ℝ E] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ E]
  [TopologicalSpace F] [IsTopologicalAddGroup F] [ContinuousSMul ℝ F] [LocallyConvexSpace ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip] {f : E → EReal}

/-- Let $E$ and $F$ be locally convex real topological vector spaces with a pairing that is
compatible in both directions, and let $f$ be a closed proper convex function on $E$. Then the
polar of the recession cone of $f$ is the closure of the convex cone generated by
$\operatorname{dom} f^*$:
$$\{y \mid \langle x, y \rangle \le 0 \text{ for all } x \text{ with } (f0^+)(x) \le 0\} =
  \operatorname{cl}\bigl(\operatorname{cone}(\operatorname{dom} f^*)\bigr).$$
This is part of Theorem 14.2 of [rockafellar1970convex].

The proof takes polars in `ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_eq_polarCone_convexDom_convexConj` and
applies the bipolar theorem. Compatibility in both directions is `IsCompatiblePairing B`
together with `IsCompatiblePairing B.flip`. -/
theorem polarCone_recessionConeFn (hf : ClosedProperConvexFn f) :
    polarCone B (recessionConeFn f)
      = closure (PointedCone.hull ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) : Set F) := by
  set K : PointedCone ℝ F := PointedCone.hull ℝ (convexDom (convexConj B f)) with hK
  rw [recessionConeFn_eq_polarCone_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) hf, ← polarCone_hull B.flip,
    ← hK]
  exact polarCone_polarCone (B := B.flip) (K : ConvexCone ℝ F).convex
    (isCone_coe_pointedCone K) ⟨0, K.zero_mem⟩

end RecessionPolarBipolar

/-! ### Bounded level sets -/

section BoundedLevel

variable {E F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
  [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F] [FiniteDimensional ℝ F]
  {B : E →ₗ[ℝ] F →ₗ[ℝ] ℝ} [IsCompatiblePairing B] [IsCompatiblePairing B.flip]

omit [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] in
/-- Let $E$ be a real normed space and $F$ a finite-dimensional real normed space, with a pairing
that is compatible in both directions. Let $D \subseteq F$ be a nonempty convex set. Then
$0 \in \operatorname{int} D$ if and only if the polar cone of $D$ is trivial:
$$0 \in \operatorname{int} D \iff \{x \in E \mid \langle x, y \rangle \le 0 \text{ for all } y
  \in D\} = \{0\}.$$

Nonemptiness is needed: for $D = \emptyset$ and trivial spaces $E$ and $F$, the polar cone is
$\{0\}$ while the interior is empty. Both directions use the criterion for interior points by
absorbing sets. In the forward direction, absorption at the origin makes every value of the
pairing vanish, and the pairing separates points. In the reverse direction, the bipolar theorem
turns a trivial polar cone into the density of the cone generated by $D$, and the relative
interior of a closure gives absorption again. -/
theorem zero_mem_interior_iff_polarCone_eq_zero {D : Set F} (hD : Convex ℝ D) (hne : D.Nonempty) :
    (0 : F) ∈ interior D ↔ polarCone B.flip D = {(0 : E)} := by
  have hconv : Convex ℝ ((PointedCone.hull ℝ D : PointedCone ℝ F) : Set F) :=
    ((PointedCone.hull ℝ D : PointedCone ℝ F) : ConvexCone ℝ F).convex
  constructor
  · intro hint
    have habs := (Convex.mem_interior_iff_absorbs hD).1 hint
    refine subset_antisymm (fun x hx => ?_) ?_
    · have key : ∀ w : F, B x w ≤ 0 := by
        intro w
        obtain ⟨ε, hε, hmem⟩ := habs w
        have hx' := hx _ (show ε • w ∈ D by simpa using hmem)
        rw [LinearMap.flip_apply, map_smul, smul_eq_mul] at hx'
        nlinarith
      have hall : ∀ y : F, B x y = 0 := by
        intro y
        have h1 := key y
        have h2 := key (-y)
        rw [map_neg] at h2
        linarith
      have hx0 : x = 0 := SeparatingDual.eq_zero_of_forall_dual_eq_zero fun g => by
        obtain ⟨y, hy⟩ := exists_pairing_eq B g
        rw [hy x]
        exact hall y
      simpa using hx0
    · rintro x rfl
      intro y _
      simp
  · intro hpol
    have hbip : closure ((PointedCone.hull ℝ D : PointedCone ℝ F) : Set F) = Set.univ := by
      have h := polarCone_polarCone (B := B.flip)
        (K := ((PointedCone.hull ℝ D : PointedCone ℝ F) : Set F))
        hconv (isCone_coe_pointedCone _) ⟨0, Submodule.zero_mem _⟩
      rw [polarCone_hull, hpol] at h
      rw [← h]
      ext x'
      simp [polarCone]
    have hfull : ((PointedCone.hull ℝ D : PointedCone ℝ F) : Set F) = Set.univ := by
      have hri : ri ((PointedCone.hull ℝ D : PointedCone ℝ F) : Set F) = Set.univ := by
        rw [← Convex.relint_closure hconv, hbip,
          intrinsicInterior_eq_interior (s := (Set.univ : Set F)) (by simp), interior_univ]
      exact subset_antisymm (Set.subset_univ _) (hri ▸ intrinsicInterior_subset)
    have hmemhull : ∀ y : F, y = 0 ∨ ∃ t : ℝ, 0 < t ∧ y ∈ t • D := fun y =>
      (mem_coe_hull_iff_of_convex hD).1 (hfull ▸ Set.mem_univ y)
    have hzero : (0 : F) ∈ D := by
      obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := hne
      rcases hmemhull (-d) with h0 | ⟨t, ht, e, he, hed⟩
      · rwa [show d = 0 from by simpa using congrArg Neg.neg h0] at hd
      · have hcomb := hD hd he (by positivity : (0 : ℝ) ≤ 1 / (1 + t))
          (by positivity : (0 : ℝ) ≤ t / (1 + t)) (by field_simp)
        have hval : (1 / (1 + t)) • d + (t / (1 + t)) • e = (0 : F) := by
          have h1 : (t / (1 + t)) • e = (1 / (1 + t)) • (t • e) := by
            rw [smul_smul]
            congr 1
            field_simp
          have hed' : t • e = -d := hed
          rw [h1, hed', smul_neg, add_neg_cancel]
        rwa [hval] at hcomb
    rw [Convex.mem_interior_iff_absorbs hD]
    intro y
    rcases hmemhull y with rfl | ⟨t, ht, e, he, hey⟩
    · exact ⟨1, one_pos, by simpa using hzero⟩
    · refine ⟨t⁻¹, by positivity, ?_⟩
      have hey' : t • e = y := hey
      rw [zero_add, ← hey', smul_smul, inv_mul_cancel₀ ht.ne', one_smul]
      exact he

/-- Let $E$ and $F$ be finite-dimensional real normed spaces with a pairing that is compatible in
both directions, and let $f$ be a closed proper convex function on $E$. Then every sublevel set
$\{z \mid f(z) \le \alpha\}$, $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$, is bounded if and only if $0 \in
\operatorname{int}(\operatorname{dom} f^*)$.

The recession cone of $f$ is the polar cone of $\operatorname{dom} f^*$
(`ConvexAnalysis.recessionConeFn_eq_polarCone_convexDom_convexConj`), every nonempty sublevel
set has this recession cone, and a nonempty closed convex set is bounded if and only if its
recession cone is $\{0\}$. The three properties of $f$ are separate hypotheses:
`ConvexAnalysis.ConvexFn f`, `ConvexAnalysis.ClosedConvex f` and
`ConvexAnalysis.ProperConvex f`. -/
theorem isBounded_setOf_le_iff_zero_mem_interior_convexDom_convexConj {f : E → EReal}
    (hf : ConvexFn f) (hcl : ClosedConvex f) (hp : ProperConvex f) :
    (∀ α : ℝ, Bornology.IsBounded {z : E | f z ≤ (α : EReal)})
      ↔ (0 : F) ∈ interior (convexDom (convexConj B f)) := by
  have hpc : ProperConvex (convexConj B f) := properConvex_convexConj ⟨hf, hcl, hp⟩
  have hepi : IsClosed (epi f) := ClosedProperConvexFn.isClosed_epi ⟨hf, hcl, hp⟩
  have hlev : ∀ α : ℝ, IsClosed {z : E | f z ≤ (α : EReal)} := fun α =>
    lowerSemicontinuous_iff_isClosed_le.1 (lowerSemicontinuous_iff_isClosed_epi.2 hepi) α
  rw [zero_mem_interior_iff_polarCone_eq_zero (B := B) (convexFn_convexConj B f).convex_convexDom
      hpc.convexDom_nonempty,
    ← recessionConeFn_eq_polarCone_convexDom_convexConj (B := B) ⟨hf, hcl, hp⟩]
  constructor
  · intro hbd
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := hp.convexDom_nonempty
    obtain ⟨α, hα⟩ := EReal.exists_coe_of_ne_bot_of_lt_top (hp.ne_bot x) hx
    have hlevne : {z : E | f z ≤ (α : EReal)}.Nonempty := ⟨x, le_of_eq hα⟩
    rw [← recessionCone_setOf_le hf hepi hlevne]
    exact (isBounded_iff_recessionCone_eq_zero (hf.convex_le _) (hlev α) hlevne).1 (hbd α)
  · intro hrec α
    rcases Set.eq_empty_or_nonempty {z : E | f z ≤ (α : EReal)} with hem | hlevne
    · rw [hem]
      exact Bornology.isBounded_empty
    · refine (isBounded_iff_recessionCone_eq_zero (hf.convex_le _) (hlev α) hlevne).2 ?_
      rw [recessionCone_setOf_le hf hepi hlevne]
      exact hrec

end BoundedLevel

end ConvexAnalysis
